To deploy to production:

1. Check open PRs (on your dev machine, in the repo, with gh logged in):

     ruby script/prerelease.rb            # dry run: preview

   The output starts with a report of the open, ready-for-review PRs,
   grouped by what each PR's review: label allows (see
   .claude/rules/review_types.md):

   - "Can merge now": PRs whose review type allows merging -- blocker
     and urgent PRs, standard PRs 24 hours after they were marked
     ready (or earlier once approved), and approved needs-review PRs.
     In general, merge everything here as part of the deploy. Use
     judgment on a PR noted "CI failing" or "conflicts": sometimes a PR
     merges with failing CI, and conflicts must be resolved first.
   - "Waiting": PRs not yet allowed to merge, with the reason --
     "can merge in Nh", "needs approval", "changes requested", a
     review-label problem, or "merge state unknown - rerun". GitHub
     works out merge conflicts lazily; the script re-queries once, and
     if a PR still shows unknown, rerun the script shortly.
   - PRs with no review label are listed at the end and treated as
     standard.

   Each line gives the PR's type, when it was marked ready, its
   author, approval, and CI state. The report only advises: nothing on
   GitHub blocks a merge. "ruby script/open_pull_requests.rb" prints
   the report alone.

2. Pre-release, once the PRs going out are merged:

     ruby script/prerelease.rb --apply    # push branch + create/update PR

   Deploys are scheduled for 8am Eastern (5am Pacific) -- 12:00 UTC
   during daylight saving time, 13:00 UTC otherwise. By default the
   script targets the next one; --at 'YYYY-MM-DD' picks another day at
   that time, and --now or --at 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM' (UTC) an exact time.

   This builds a draft PR from the changelog-pending branch holding the
   next CHANGELOG.md section (its heading names the tag the deploy will
   create) and article_pending.textile (the rows the deploy publishes
   to the MO Article changelog). The CHANGELOG.md section lists every
   PR merged since the last deploy (except the changelog PRs
   themselves); the Article rows come from the changelog blocks in the
   PR bodies. PRs without a changelog block are listed in the PR body
   for an article yes/no verdict. After the open-PR report, the output
   lists any review: blocker or urgent PRs going out in this deploy.

3. Announce the release in the site banner. --apply ends by printing a
   line like:

     Next release Sep 15 noon UTC (8am eastern, 5am pacific). See "pre-release change log":https://github.com/MushroomObserver/mushroom-observer/blob/changelog-pending/CHANGELOG.md for details<br/>

   (In winter the UTC time reads "13:00 UTC"; the local times stay the
   same.)

   Replace the first line of the banner with it at
   https://mushroomobserver.org/admin/banners and leave the other
   lines as they are. This is a manual step because the script runs on
   a dev machine, not the production server. Saving creates a new
   banner version, so the banner shows again to visitors who dismissed
   the last one.

4. Review the changelog PR. The deploy publishes article_pending.textile
   as merged, so any desired corrections or additions (e.g. promoting a
   blockless PR) can be made to that file in the PR -- usually none
   are needed. If more PRs merge before the deploy, run
   "ruby script/prerelease.rb --apply" again to refresh it. Merge it
   as the LAST PR before deploying.

5. Deploy:

     ssh mushroomobserver.org
     sudo su mo
     cd /var/web/mo
     script/deploy.sh

   The deploy tags the release with the pre-release's tag name,
   inserts the Article rows below the Article's table header, and then
   replaces the first line of the site banner with one of:

     Sep 15 release completed at 12:04 UTC. See "MO Improvements":https://mushroomobserver.org/articles/55 for details
     Sep 15 release completed at 12:04 UTC. No user-facing changes - "details here":https://github.com/MushroomObserver/mushroom-observer/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md

   The first when article_pending.textile has rows, the second when it
   is empty. The time is the server's clock, in UTC, when the banner is
   set -- not the time in the deploy tag -- and the rest of the banner
   is kept. If the Article or banner step fails, the deploy still
   succeeds; retry on the server with:

     bundle exec rails runner script/update_article_changelog.rb --apply
     bundle exec rails runner script/update_release_banner.rb --apply

   Both are dry runs without --apply.

Blocker deploys: follow the same steps with
"ruby script/prerelease.rb --apply --now", which targets the current
time instead of the next 8am Eastern, then deploy as soon as the
changelog PR is merged. The Article and banner update as usual.

Forced deploys (rare): with no undeployed pre-release on main,
deploy.sh warns and asks whether to force. Force only when something
can't wait even for a blocker deploy, or the pre-release or deploy
scripts are broken. Forcing deploys main as-is -- no CHANGELOG.md
section and no MO Article update; the skipped PRs roll into the next
pre-release/deploy cycle. The banner's first line becomes:

  Undocumented forced deploy occurred Sep 17 14:32 UTC

Retry that on the server with:

  bundle exec rails runner script/update_release_banner.rb --forced --apply

Asset-only icon refresh: script/deploy.sh --icons-only (no code deploy,
no pre-release needed); --icons bundles the refresh into a normal
deploy.

The MO Article id (55) and the banner wording live in
script/release_notes.rb; bump the id there when a new year's Article
is created.

Design and history: issues #5155 and #5381.
